l towards a  more concerted military action but what  it is is a recognition that this is an  organization that is a direct threat to  our country I mean if we think about  what happened in Afghanistan there we  had a Taliban regime that played host to  al-qaeda here in the heart of Iraq we've  actually got a terrorist organization  running a potential state with all the  resources and money that involves  look we have to take a long-term tough  intelligent patient view of this we're  going to have to squeeze it out of  existence it's going to take time we  start with the measures we take in our  own country to keep ourselves safe we  then work with partners to put the  pressure on this organization we're  helping to get arms to the Kurds we're  helping to get in Iraqi government whose  real responsibility it is to put the  squeeze on this organization and then we  work with allies and partners to ask  what more we can do use the phrase put  the squeeze is the aim to destroy is yes  no doubt the world would be a safer  place if we could squeeze this  organization out of existence what there  ought to be in Iraq is a democratic  Iraqi government that looks after all of  its people Sunnis Shia and Kurd what  they ought to be in Syria instead of a  conflict between a brutal dictator and  an opposition it is a democratic  government in Syria that can represent  all its people in the long term those  are the only answers it's a mixture of  the action we take and political  solutions on the ground but you cannot  deal with is without targeting their HQ  in Syria and how do you do that with  President Assad or without his tacit  support what as I say what we need is  that combination of working with those  on the ground who are fighting ISIL in  Iraq that is the Kurds and the Iraqi  government in Syria it's the official  Syrian opposition we need to work with  them obviously we also need to engage  with the regional partners and players  some sort of Western intervention t